Maria G: Heads-up — after the graph-capture refactor deployed last night I'm seeing a clear throughput regression on our 2x8gpu tensor-parallel runs. ~20% lower tokens/s and lots of all-reduce stalls.
Jon K: Do you have a trace? I ran a quick nvprof timeline and the overlap drops around the KV prefetch phase.
Maria G: I attached the perf-bot trace (short summary below). When capture is enabled, stream ordering seems different and an event that used to be waited on is missing during replay.
perf-bot: Profiling summary: increased idle time on compute stream (+180ms), repeated cudaStreamWaitEvent calls during capture boundary, NCCL all-reduce queues stalled. Sampled kernel selection unchanged.
Sam R: I think KV prefetch now issues `cudaEventRecord` on the prefetch stream but that event is not properly replayed inside the CUDA graph. When the graph is instantiated/replayed the event either isn't present or the wait happens on the wrong stream.
Tia L: Reproable in my env with `export REDWOOD_RUNTIME_CAPTURE=1`. Minimal repro below (pseudo):
```
// prefetch on stream A
cudaEventRecord(evt, streamA);
// capture on stream B
cudaStreamBeginCapture(streamB, cudaStreamCaptureModeGlobal);
cudaStreamWaitEvent(streamB, evt, 0);
launch_kernel_on_B(...);
cudaStreamEndCapture(streamB, &graph);
cudaGraphInstantiate(&instance, graph, ...);
cudaGraphLaunch(instance, streamB);
```
On replay the `evt` behavior diverges because it wasn't part of the graph capture.
Jon K: That aligns with what I saw. The old code moved prefetch onto the same stream as compute; the refactor tried to keep prefetch off the hot path and erroneously left event semantics outside capture. When capture records, external events need special handling (cudaGraphAddEventRecordNode / wait node). We never added those nodes.
Maria G: Right — we treated prefetch event as a lightweight signal but inside graph replay it's not recreated. Result: compute proceeds before KV is ready -> cache miss + extra all-reduce overhead.
Sam R: Short-term fix: force KV prefetch onto compute stream for captured runs (fall back to old behavior if REDWOOD_RUNTIME_CAPTURE=1). Patch suggestion:
```
if (capture_enabled) prefetch_stream = compute_stream;
else prefetch_stream = background_stream;
```
Jon K: That should be quick. Longer-term we should add explicit graph event nodes so prefetch can stay off-stream. Adding graph addEventRecord/wait nodes during capture is a bit fiddly but more correct.
Tia L: I can prototype a graph-node approach. Two gotchas: (1) event affinity across devices for TP/PP setups, (2) ensuring instantiated graph includes event semantics for replay on different streams.
perf-bot: Quick numbers after local revert-of-prefetch-to-compute: throughput returned to baseline within noise (+/-2%).
Maria G: OK, let's do the quick revert on the canary and roll forward the graph-node fix behind a flag.
infra-bot: Deploy note: I rolled back runtime-graph-refactor on canary (canary-3) at 1805002500. Monitoring.
Jon K: I'll open a PR with the revert and a TODO for graph event nodes (link in PR description). Assigning Sam and Tia for follow-up.
Sam R: I'll take the revert PR review and add a small repro test to catch this class of errors in CI (capture mode + prefetch stream mismatch).
Tia L: I'll start the graph-node prototype and post a short design doc in #eng-runtime.
Maria G: Thanks all. Also adding a short perf test to Redwood Optimize to detect regressions when capture mode toggles kernel/stream placement. :thumbsup:
